{
 "id": "2026-11-16-a",
 "title": "12 colour sensors... and worse colour vision than you 🦐",
 "description": "Mantis shrimp have 12 kinds of colour receptor; you have 3. So they must see colours we can't imagine? In a 2014 test (University of Queensland, published in Science), mantis shrimp were trained to pick a coloured light for food. When two colours were about 15-25 nm apart they could no longer tell them apart, while humans can split colours roughly 5 nm apart. Researchers think they recognise colours directly from the pattern of all 12 receptors (fast, like a barcode scanner) instead of comparing channels like we do.\n\nSources: Thoen et al., Science (2014); University of Queensland; National Geographic.",
 "tags": [
